{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1788265903,"nonce":"McBG1JktkGlhAd3kmm5rAekvX5s=","title":"threat-pardon reports success with refused_count 0 when every pardon lapsed on arrival","message":"Found on the live bench, session 13 (`20260901T121508`), testing the pardon path that the smoke pass could not reach because that map had no hostiles. **The mechanism is correct. The reporting is not.**\n\n## What happened\n\nThree megascarabs spawned, then pardoned with a reason:\n\n    threat-pardon {\"ids\":[49470,49471,49472],\"reason\":\"dormant megascarabs, …\"}\n\n    ok: true\n    refused_count: 0\n    pardons_held: 3\n    hostiles: 3   hostiles_pardoned: 0   hostiles_unpardoned: 3\n\nThe call reported success, refused nothing, and **`hostiles_unpardoned` did not move.** The listing afterwards explains why — every entry is `pardoned:false, dormant:false, lapsed:true` — but only if you make a second call and read a field the first response never mentioned.\n\n## Why it is correct, and still wrong\n\nThe lapse rule is right and must NOT change: a pardon lapses when the subject wakes, keyed on `LordJob_StructureThreatCluster`'s graph starting in `LordToil_Sleep` with every wake transition leaving it. My megascarabs were spawned awake, so the pardons lapsed the instant they were recorded. The verb did exactly what it says on the tin.\n\nThe defect is that **`refused_count: 0` is true and misleading at the same time.** Nothing was refused. Nothing took effect either. The distinction between \"your pardon is in force\" and \"your pardon was recorded and is already dead\" is the entire value of the field, and the add response does not draw it.\n\nThis is the same shape as the two findings this session already fixed. `dev:spawn-thing` returned `ok:true` with `placed:0` and the reason lived only in a field nobody printed (M1 finding A). `research-set` returned `bench_ok:true` with no bench, and that lie hid A for five in-game days. A verb that succeeds while achieving nothing, with the explanation one call away, is the house failure mode.\n\n## Scope\n\nDo NOT change the lapse rule or the digest's pruning discipline — both are right, and the digest correctly flags rather than prunes.\n\nChange the ADD response so the outcome is legible in the call that produced it:\n\n- Report how many of the submitted ids took effect versus lapsed immediately, as distinct counts. `pardons_held` conflates them.\n- Say WHY per id, reusing the `lapsed` reason the listing already computes — the caller should not have to make a second call to learn its first one did nothing.\n- Decide whether pardoning an already-awake hostile should be a REFUSAL rather than a lapsed record. There is a real argument either way: refusing is louder and matches \"a pardon is a decision about something dormant\", while recording-then-lapsing preserves the intent in the journal for the post-mortem to read. Whichever is chosen, the response must state it.\n- `refused_count` needs either a companion `lapsed_count` or a rename, because as it stands a caller reading it as \"everything went through\" is reading it correctly and being misled.\n\nThe journal row is already good and should not change: seq 28 carried the ids and the reason as a `type:action` row, which is what makes the decision auditable later.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- Pardoning an awake hostile reports, in that same response, that the pardon did not take effect and why.\n- Pardoning a genuinely dormant hostile moves `hostiles_pardoned` / `hostiles_unpardoned` and says so. **This path is still unproven live** — the session-13 bench had no dormant cluster and one could not be constructed on it; prove it with a `world-fixture` or a map that generates a sleeping cluster.\n- A mixed batch (some dormant, some awake) reports both outcomes per id rather than one aggregate verdict.\n- `accept/s13-mod-surface.py` gains the case.","files":null}]}